\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Invariant tensors count the gauge orbits}
\date{2026-11-03}

\p{Let #{G} be a finite group, with unitary irreducible
representatives as in the [surface data](fdip-006V). Let
#{s,h:E\to V} be a [finite incidence presentation](fdip-0002);
loops and parallel edges are allowed, and either set may be
empty. The [gauge action](fdip-006E) of #{G^V} on connections
#{U\in G^E} is
##{(g\cdot U)_e=g_{h(e)}U_eg_{s(e)}^{-1}.}
A function #{F:G^E\to\mathbb C} is gauge invariant when
#{F(g\cdot U)=F(U)} for every #{g}. The dimension of the
gauge-invariant functions equals the number of orbits of this
action. It also equals
##{\sum_{\rho}\prod_{x\in V}\dim W_x(\rho)^G,}
where the sum runs over all assignments of irreducible
representatives to the edges. For each assignment, the local
space at a vertex #{x} is the tensor product of the
representation spaces of the edges leaving #{x} and of the dual
spaces of the edges entering #{x}. An empty tensor product is
the scalar line. The group element #{g_x} acts by
#{\rho_e(g_x)^{-1}} on each outgoing factor and by
#{(\,g_x\cdot\lambda)(v)=\lambda(\rho_e(g_x)v)} on each incoming
covector. A basis of the invariant tensors in these local
spaces, as the edge assignment varies, pushes to a basis of the
gauge-invariant functions.}

\p{The datum of an edge assignment together with one invariant
tensor at each vertex is the finite spin-network datum of this
note. Nothing proved here identifies that datum with a continuous
orthonormal basis, or with the
[charged multiplicity spaces](fdip-008B).}

\proof{
\p{Two connections lie in the same orbit exactly when one is the
image of the other under #{G^V}. A gauge-invariant function is
constant on each orbit, and the indicator of an orbit is
gauge invariant. Those indicators are linearly independent and
span the invariant functions, so the dimension equals the number
of orbits.}

\p{The identity #{\sum_\rho d_\rho^2=|G|} is the evaluation at
the identity in the [handle convolution](fdip-00A8). The matrix
orthogonality derived there makes the #{|G|} matrix coefficients
of the irreducible representatives linearly independent as
functions on #{G}. They are therefore a basis of all functions
on #{G}. Products of basis functions, one factor for each edge,
are a basis of the functions on #{G^E}. Grouping that basis by
the edge assignment writes every function uniquely as a sum of
its assignment components. The gauge action preserves each
component: conjugating an edge value by group elements keeps the
result in the matrix-coefficient span of the same
representation.}

\p{Fix an edge assignment #{\rho}. Send an elementary tensor
#{\bigotimes_e(v_e\otimes\lambda_e)} to the function
##{F(U)=\prod_{e\in E}\lambda_e(\rho_e(U_e)v_e),}
and extend linearly. On basis vectors this produces every product
of matrix coefficients of the assigned representations, so the
map is an isomorphism from #{\bigotimes_e(V_e\otimes V_e^*)} onto
the assignment component. Substituting the gauge action gives
##{F(g\cdot U)
 =\prod_e\lambda_e\bigl(\rho_e(g_{h(e)})
 \rho_e(U_e)\rho_e(g_{s(e)})^{-1}v_e\bigr).}
The right-hand side is the function of the tensor whose vector
at #{e} is #{\rho_e(g_{s(e)})^{-1}v_e} and whose covector at
#{e} is #{g_{h(e)}\cdot\lambda_e}. Reordering tensor factors
assigns each vector factor to the source vertex and each
covector factor to the head vertex. Under that reordering the
isomorphism is equivariant for #{G^V}, and the action of
#{g_x} touches only the factors assigned to #{x}.}

\p{Averaging over the group element at one vertex is a projector
onto the tensors fixed by that vertex, and it acts on the factor
belonging to that vertex. The average over #{G^V} is the
composition of these commuting projectors, so its image is the
tensor product of the local invariant spaces. The dimension of
the invariant tensors in one assignment is therefore the product
of the local invariant dimensions. A function is gauge invariant
exactly when every assignment component comes from an invariant
tensor. Summing the dimensions gives the stated count, and
bases of the local invariant spaces push to a basis of the
gauge-invariant functions.}
}
